.TE
.PP
\f[CR]command\f[R] is the only required key.
A utility runs until you close it (\f[B]tum\-close\f[R](1)); there is no
exit\-policy key.
.SS ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES
Any \f[CR]key:value\f[R] line that is not a special key is an
environment variable: the key is the variable name, the value is its
value.
One variable per line, no \f[CR]env\f[R] prefix, no comma separation.
.IP
.EX
PGPASSWORD: secret
FOO: bar
.EE
.PP
A value may contain any character, including \f[CR],\f[R] and
\f[CR]=\f[R].
.PP
At launch, the spawned process inherits the calling process\[cq]s
environment, then the file\[cq]s environment keys are applied on top
(file values win over inherited).
The \f[CR]use\-process\-env\f[R] key changes that order for keys that
also exist in the calling environment:
.IP
.EX
PGUSER: postgres
PGHOST: localhost
use\-process\-env: yes
.EE
.PP
With \f[CR]use\-process\-env: yes\f[R], if the user has
\f[CR]PGUSER\f[R] exported, that value is used; otherwise
\f[CR]postgres\f[R] from the file is used.
The file provides defaults; the live environment wins for variables the
user has set.
Environment values are static [em] they never contain
\f[CR]{{placeholder}}\f[R] tokens.
.SS THE INPUT KEY
Some utilities need an argument the user supplies at create time [em] a
location for a weather report, a file path for a log tail, a query for a
database console.
The \f[CR]input\f[R] key declares these; \f[CR]args\f[R],
\f[CR]cwd\f[R], and \f[CR]command\f[R] reference them with
\f[CR]{{name}}\f[R] placeholders.
\f[CR]input\f[R] is repeatable; each line declares one placeholder:
.IP
.EX
command: curl
args: \[dq]\-s wttr.in/{{location}}?format={{unit}}\[dq]
input: location
input: unit
.EE
.PP
At create time (\f[B]tum\-new\f[R](1), or the create branch of
\f[B]tum\-borrow\f[R](1)), every \f[CR]input\f[R] value is prompted in
declaration order \f[B]before\f[R] any tmux mutation, then substituted
into every \f[CR]{{name}}\f[R] token in \f[CR]args\f[R], \f[CR]cwd\f[R],
and \f[CR]command\f[R].
\f[B]tum\-borrow\f[R] that reuses an existing instance never prompts
[em] inputs are collected only when a new instance is created.
If standard input is not a TTY (non\-interactive mode), the command
fails with the names of the missing inputs rather than hanging.
.PP
Secret values are supplied interactively or via a secret provider, never
stored in definition files; secret prompts read without echoing.
.SS LAYOUT FILE KEYS
A layout file is flat \f[CR]key:value\f[R], parsed by the same loop as a
utility file.
Two keys are special:
.PP
.TS
