Meaning
T}
_
T{
\f[CR]layout_id\f[R]
T}@T{
no
T}@T{
Layout id stamped on member panes.
Defaults to the filename.
T}
T{
\f[CR]focus\f[R]
T}@T{
no
T}@T{
Which member gets focus when the layout is borrowed.
The value is a member name, or \f[CR]host\f[R] to focus the host pane.
Defaults to \f[CR]host\f[R].
T}
.TE
.PP
Every other key is a \f[B]member entry\f[R] (see LAYOUT DSL).
There is no \f[CR]members:\f[R] wrapper and no indentation.
.SS LAYOUT DSL
Every non\-special key in a layout file is a member entry.
The key is the member name (it must match a utility definition
filename); the value is its placement:
.IP
.EX
<member\-name>: anchor=<host|member\-name>, side=<right|left|top|bottom>, size=<N%|Nc>
.EE
.PP
A complete layout file:
.IP
.EX
layout_id: dev
focus: editor
editor: anchor=host, side=right, size=60%
term: anchor=host, side=bottom, size=20%
logs: anchor=term, side=bottom, size=30%
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]anchor\f[R] defaults to \f[CR]host\f[R].
A member may anchor to another member, allowing two panes on the same
side of the host (above, \f[CR]logs\f[R] anchors below \f[CR]term\f[R],
so both sit on the bottom while \f[CR]editor\f[R] takes the right).
\f[CR]side\f[R] is required.
\f[CR]size\f[R] is a percentage of the anchor or a cell count; it is
optional.
.SS EXAMPLES
A minimal utility:
.IP
.EX
command: htop
slot: 1
.EE
.PP
A log viewer prompted for its file at create time, with pager defaults
overridable from the shell:
.IP
.EX
command: tail
args: \-n 500 \-f {{logfile}}
input: logfile
cwd: \[ti]/projects/acme/var/log
slot: l
use\-process\-env: yes
PAGER: less
LESS: \-RiX
.EE
.PP
A three\-member layout:
.IP
.EX
layout_id: dev
focus: editor
editor: anchor=host, side=right, size=60%
term: anchor=host, side=bottom, size=20%
logs: anchor=term, side=bottom, size=30%
.EE
